---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const findings = [
  {
    n: '01',
    title: 'The first attention-allocation study stopped before outcomes were opened.',
    status: 'negative feasibility result',
    copy: 'The study reconstructed 1,815 usable ICLR 2020 papers, but that sample was too small to distinguish the planned effects with enough precision. The citation outcomes stayed sealed rather than weakening the test after seeing the answer.',
    survived: 'A clean negative result and a better estimate of how much data a useful successor study needs.',
    href: 'https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/research/attention_allocation/t0/TRACK_A_V01_FEASIBILITY_RESULT.md',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    title: 'The larger follow-up is limited by trustworthy historical data.',
    status: 'open constraint',
    copy: 'The available unbiased historical sources still fall short of the planned sample. A convenient 2021 source was rejected because its availability depended on the historical decision, which would introduce selection bias.',
    survived: 'The next problem is finding a neutral historical cohort, not writing more analysis code.',
    href: 'https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/research/attention_allocation/t0/TRACK_A_V02_SOURCE_FEASIBILITY_RESULT.md',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    title: 'Optimising for accepted papers can discard disagreement.',
    status: 'descriptive result',
    copy: 'In an early expert-attention benchmark using a 20% review budget, ranking by post-review score preserved many historically accepted papers but preserved very few high-disagreement cases.',
    survived: 'A useful allocation policy must preserve more than agreement with a past institutional decision.',
    href: 'https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/docs/EXPERT_ATTENTION_PHASE1_FINDINGS.md',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    title: 'Generic “epistemic handoff” is not a new institution.',
    status: 'claim narrowed',
    copy: 'Existing work on knowledge transfer, brokerage, absorptive capacity and expert evaluation already covers much of the general problem of moving knowledge toward people able to assess or use it.',
    survived: 'A narrower question: can a concrete handoff protocol make missing expertise and changes in confidence easier to track?',
    href: '/drafts/epistemic-handoff/',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    title: 'A distinct new economics of epistemic abundance has not yet been established.',
    status: 'open question',
    copy: 'Established economics already contains strong accounts of bottlenecks, complements, production constraints, innovation and rebound effects.',
    survived: 'An empirical question about how bottlenecks, value and rents move when a previously expensive capability becomes cheap.',
    href: '/research/',
  },
  {
    n: '06',
    title: 'Nemosyne is useful as a bounded testbed, not as proof of the whole programme.',
    status: 'boundary established',
    copy: 'Software experiments can test specific questions about representation, analyst performance and discovery workflows. They cannot by themselves establish broader economic or institutional claims.',
    survived: 'Use Nemosyne where the intervention and outcome can actually be observed and measured.',
    href: 'https://nemosyne.world',
  },
  {
    n: '07',
    title: 'The public ICLR 2021 archive route is now closed for this design.',
    status: 'source-level stop',
    copy: 'OpenReview confirmed that the requested historical PDF is not public, and a later bounded retry of the healthy Common Crawl 2020-45 index found no captures for any of ten frozen forum URLs or their ten corresponding PDF URLs.',
    survived: 'The study now moves to a prospectively frozen source-comparability design, beginning with a provenance audit of PeerRead’s ICLR 2017 paper drafts. Citation outcomes remain sealed.',
    href: 'https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/research/attention_allocation/t0/ICLR2021_COMMON_CRAWL_RETRY_RESULT.md',
  },
  {
    n: '08',
    title: 'Track A v0.2 cannot reach its source requirement without weakening the protocol.',
    status: 'pre-outcome abstain',
    copy: 'PeerRead looked like a possible ICLR 2017 source, but its preserved papers are not clean submission-time snapshots: one accepted PDF directly contains material its meta-review says was added after reviewer discussion. Once that source is excluded, the largest remaining admissible addition leaves the study at 4,547 candidates, below the frozen 4,600 floor before further losses.',
    survived: 'A terminal outcome-blind result. The citation outcomes remain sealed, and any continuation must be a separately frozen v0.3 source design rather than a relaxed interpretation of v0.2.',
    href: 'https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch/blob/main/research/attention_allocation/t0/TRACK_A_V02_TERMINAL_RESULT.md',
  },
];
---
<Layout
  title="Results"
  description="Findings, negative results and corrections from Agalmic Research, explained in plain language."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Results</p>
      <h1>What have we learned or changed our minds about?</h1>
      <p class="lede">A useful result can be positive, negative or corrective. This page records what testing, prior work and implementation have forced us to believe more narrowly than before.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell thesis">
      <p class="eyebrow">A simple rule</p>
      <blockquote>A result should change what we are willing to claim or what we do next.</blockquote>
      <p>Some items here are working research results rather than peer-reviewed findings. Their status is shown so readers can distinguish evidence from interpretation and open questions.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Current record</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>Findings and corrections so far.</h2>
          <p>The emphasis is on what changed, not on the machinery that produced it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="index-list">
        {findings.map((item) => (
          <article class="index-item">
            <span class="index-meta">R{item.n}</span>
            <div>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p>{item.copy}</p>
              <p><strong>What remains:</strong> {item.survived}</p>
              <p><a href={item.href}>Read the supporting record →</a></p>
            </div>
            <span class="index-status">{item.status}</span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Why keep negative results?</p>
        <h2>Stopping can be progress.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>If a study is too weak to answer its question, if prior work already explains a proposed idea, or if a convenient dataset would bias the result, recording that limit prevents wasted effort and overclaiming.</p>
        <p class="large">The aim is not to make every idea survive. It is to make the surviving ideas better.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href="/research/">Current research</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="/publications/">Publications</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="/lineage/">Prior work and lineage</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
